- F-34 **Against the interpreter's library, on the code of `285efad802`, the code generator refuses none of the declarations of the fourteen components that the distance stage checks; the measure's script can take the count only on a patched tree.** The setting is the distance stage's (F-53): the checker's failures caught per declaration, and the bound `Any`. Without the patch, the code generator stops `FortressLibrary`, `FortressBuiltin` and `FlatString` whole at the wrappers of their helper classes (row 874), so the measure's script first patches a scratch worktree (`compile-ladder/plan-31/PROBES.md` gives the patch). The patch is needed while the code generator's table of the classes that carry built-in types has no rows for the one library's seven number types (`payloadObjects`, `NamingCzar.java:606-614`). The script also edits `NN32`'s two getters, which changes nothing now, since the library declares them so (F-394). Then: 0 refused, 754 emitted, no component failure. The script skips the dispatch methods, so it does not reach `String`'s two calls with a type assumption at one argument (`Library/String.fss:67`, `:69`). `String`'s real compile with `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one`, on the same tree without the patch, ends clean, and both calls call the body of the `(String, String)` declaration (F-325). On the code of `ba3c7c876d` the script counted 1 refused, with 753 emitted: `__bigOperator2`, a generic method called on a receiver typed by a type variable (row 1076, fixed since, F-392). The real compile refused 2 declarations: that one, and `String`'s `concatAndBalanceIfNecessary`, which holds the two calls (row 818). A declaration counts at its first wall, and some hold a second. The measure cannot see a class that is emitted and fails when it loads. No emitted class of the measure is run, and no test checks the count, since the gate's distance stage runs no code generation. So after the switch-over the code generator still stands between the library and bytecode. Measured once on each code. Command: `explorations/compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/planner/codegen-measure.sh <scratch-worktree> <out-dir>`. Detail: `compile-ladder/plan-34/probes/measure-285efad802.txt`; `compile-ladder/plan-33/probes/measure-ba3c7c876d.txt`; `compile-ladder/plan-34/probes/rehearsal.md`, "3. The walls, in the order met", 1; `compile-ladder/plan-31/PROBES.md`, "3. The measure: the declarations of the fourteen components that the code generator refuses".
  from: reviews/batch-34-review.md, "4. The measure: the declarations of the one library that the code generator refuses"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: the count on the landed code, measured after the landing by the coordinator's worker (measure-285efad802.txt, committed 8f7604e42c): 0 refused, 754 emitted, String's real compile 'compile exit=0 seconds=36'; it supersedes the counts of the gather's two blocks, taken apart on rung T's code (38d8a44e56, 0 refused, 754 emitted) and rung P's (a95eb01926, String compiled whole). Moved here as superseded by the landed code's count: on the code of d3747846c1 the count was 13 in 4 kinds, with 745 emitted; the other 12 stopped at varargs, in two kinds (row 624), or at a closure class written twice (row 315), both fixed since (F-390, F-389); on the code of c3b16ef2d1 it was 17 in 5 kinds, with 742 emitted; the sums differed from code to code, since the earlier files also counted as emitted an object that its pre-pass refused (compile-ladder/plan-33/probes/measure-d3747846c1.txt; compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/planner/measure-c3b16ef2d1.txt). The citations of rung T's and rung P's report sections give way to measure-285efad802.txt, which cites both.

note F-240
  from: reviews/batch-34-review.md, "5. The gate, the count and the distance"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: the machine of climb batch 34's gate, which the commit stage's block of 2026-11-20 19:12Z does not give: the VM restart of 17:06 UTC on 2026-11-20 ('uptime -s' 17:06:26) gave the session another machine, an Intel Xeon at 2.30 GHz before it and at 2.10 GHz after it (the gate's '# machine' lines); testFast took 11 min 45 s against climb batch 33b's 7 min 53 s. So no stage time of this gate compares with climb batch 33b's, and none shows a change of the code's speed.

- F-325 **The code generator compiles a type assumption at a method call's receiver, at a call whose every dispatched argument is an assumption, at a call of an overloaded function or operator of its component where only some dispatched arguments are assumptions, and as a value; the other partial calls stay refused, and so does such a call where a declaration at or below the resolved one is generic or its own dispatch would hold a functional method.** At a receiver, `(e asif T).m(args)` calls the body that `T` gives `m` as a static method of its trait's `DefaultTraitMethods` class, `self` first. Where every dispatched argument of a call is an assumption, the call runs the declaration that the checker resolves, without dispatch. Where only some are, the call of an overloaded function or operator declared in the call's own component reaches the declarations at or below the resolved one whose parameter at each assumed argument accepts the assumed type and that the other arguments' static types do not exclude. It dispatches among them on the other arguments' run-time types, as walk does: one declaration reached gets a direct call; else the call takes the resolved declaration's dispatch method where that holds the same declarations, or a dispatch method of their own, in a class `<component>$asif@<span>`. Elsewhere the assumption is the value at `T`. Refused with 'Can't compile a dispatched call whose arguments are not all type assumptions': a partial call of a functional method; a partial call of an overloaded method whose receiver is an assumption too; a dotted method call with an assumption at an argument; and a partial call whose own dispatch would hold a functional method. Refused with messages of their own: a generic declaration at or below the resolved one, a generic method on an assumed receiver, an overloaded generic function. Row 818 names each refused form's `XXX` test, in its reproducer and its notes. Under walk the assumption stays on a bound value (row 919), a tuple under one cannot be taken apart (row 920), and a generic method on an assumed receiver stops (row 922); an overloaded one runs (`tests/AsIfOverloadedMethodWalk`). Source: `compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java`, `forAsIfExpr`, `assumedReceiverCall`, `assumedArgumentsCall`, `partiallyAssumedCall`; `compiler/OverloadSet.java`, `membersUnderAssumptions`, `generateDispatchAmong`. Tests: `compiler_tests/AsIfCallsCompiled`, `AsIfPartialCallCompiled`, `AsIfPartialDispatchCompiled`, `tests/AsIfCallsWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-partial-assumption/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-asif/REPORT.md`.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-partial-assumption/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: the gather's draft of 2026-11-20 17:55Z from rung P of climb batch 34, put into the record's form: the title names every compiled form and points to the refused ones; the list of eleven tests cut to the compiled ones, since row 818's notes name each refused form's XXX test (XXXAsIfPartialMethodCompiled, XXXAsIfPartialReceiverCompiled, XXXAsIfPartialDottedArgCompiled, XXXAsIfPartialWithFunctionalMethodCompiled, XXXAsIfPartialGenericCompiled, XXXAsIfGenericMethodCompiled, XXXAsIfGenericFunctionCompiled); checked against reviews/batch-34-review.md, section 1, P (CodeGen.java:7300-7303, :7326-7366; OverloadSet.java:2315-2331, :2341-2351; the refused forms at CodeGen.java:7274, :7537, :7310-7311, :7351-7352)

- F-392 **The code generator compiles a generic method called on a receiver typed by a type variable.** It casts the receiver to the variable's bound that declares or inherits the method, the most specific where several do; the receiver object's own method runs. The bounds include a `where` clause's, also that of the calling method where it has no static parameters of its own and the clause bounds its trait's or object's parameter. A bound that is another type variable is not read, since the checker refuses the call first (row 1093). Test: `compiler_tests/TypeVariableReceiverGenericMethod`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-type-variable-receiver/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-type-variable-receiver/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: the gather's draft of 2026-11-20 17:52Z from rung T of climb batch 34, put into the record's form: the title names the code generator; the where clause of a body without static parameters of its own is the skeptic's fix 54c56dc6e9 (whereClauseBodyCodeGen); the second detail section, "The owner as found, and the cast", cut, the first holding the mechanism

- F-393 **The code generator keeps a local variable in its environment for the rest of its block only, so a closure or task after an `if` arm or another nested block reads no slot of the block's bindings.** `CodeGen.forLocalVarDecl` adds a declaration's bindings to the environment of its body, after the initializer (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:4822-4828`). Before, a later closure or task whose body used the name of a binding first in an arm, even as its own binding or as a numeral's coercion parameter `r` (row 983), captured the arm's slot, unset on the path that skipped the arm: 'VerifyError: Bad local variable type' (row 1078). With the repair, in one run on a scratch copy with the switch-over rehearsal's patch (`compile-ladder/plan-34/probes/rehearsal-34.patch`) less its stand-in for row 1078, `Hello` runs against the one library with the JVM's verifier on. Over that copy's compiled components, ASM's verifier refuses none of `FortressLibrary`'s 4,694 methods, and 2 methods in all, both in `RangeInternals` (row 1054), with 1,092 template classes skipped. Test: `compiler_tests/BlockBindingScopeCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-label-local-verify/REPORT.md`, "The cause, by the bytecode" and "The repair in the scratch copy".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-label-local-verify/REPORT.md, "The repair in the scratch copy"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: the gather's draft of 2026-11-20 17:56Z from rung L of climb batch 34, put into the record's form: the verifier's count bounded to its one scratch run and given whole ('TOTAL classes 1092 methods 9477 refused 2 templates skipped 1092', REPORT.md:115; reviews/batch-34-review.md, section 1, L); row 1078's site, the one library's simplestRationalBetween, left to the row

- F-394 **The compiled checker checks the return type of a getter declared in a trait or an object against the getter that it keeps from being inherited, but not in an object expression (row 1097).** At the same parameter types, a pair of getters is checked by the Return Type Rule as a pair of methods is (`returnTypeCheck`'s message, 'should be a subtype of the'), each getter's receiver read as the plain type that declares it (P-146, P-157). So a narrowing getter below a self-typed trait compiles, and a widening one is refused, also where its return type is inferred. A dotted method so redeclared below a self-typed trait still reads the trait's receiver as the intersection and is refused where it narrows, a departure from P-146 and P-157 (row 1094). An object expression's getter or dotted method is not checked so; the code generator then stops at 'Can't compile ObjectExpr' (row 375), so no compiled program reaches it. In the one library's fourteen components no getter widens: `NN32`'s `zero` and `one` and thirteen more getter declarations answer their inherited types, so the count and the distance stay 0. Its other components still hold some (row 1095). Test: `compiler_tests/XXXGetterReturnTypeNotSubtype`; `compiler_tests/XXXInferredGetterReturnTypeNotSubtype`; `compiler_tests/SelfTypedGetterNarrowedReturn`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-widening-getter/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-widening-getter/SKEPTIC.md`, "My programs, old code against new".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-widening-getter/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; from: reviews/batch-34-review.md, "1. The landed changes against the decisions, rung by rung and path by path"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: the gather's draft of 2026-11-20 17:58Z and the merged-diff review's of 18:59Z from rung G of climb batch 34, put into the record's form; the title bounded to declarations by row 1097, opened by G's skeptic ('fortress typecheck' exits 0 at 355edea65c and on the base; OverloadingChecker.scala:377-378, :422); the library's half of rung G added, which closed row 993 (FortressBuiltin.fss:264-265; G.reserved.1); with it, under walk, a rank-2 or rank-3 range's indexValuePairs takes a subscript, where the base stopped with 'Cannot find definition for method _[_]' (tests/RangeBodiesWalk.fss:17-20), and ArrayList's extractLeft makes a Just[\(E,List[\E\])\]; moved here, as what the code did before the change: a widening getter below a self-typed trait stopped the code generator ('malformed overload set not rejected'), one whose return type is inferred compiled to a class that fails the JVM's verification ('VerifyError: Bad return type'), and the others compiled

- F-225 **In a self-typed trait with no `comprises` clause, or one that lists only `...`, the compiled checker types `self` in the method bodies as `C[\X\] & X`, the trait met with the implementing type, read from the trait's F-bound; the methods' signatures keep `self` at the plain trait type `C[\X\]`, save in the overloading check's exclusion test (F-277) and in the Return Type check of a dotted method redeclared below the trait (row 1094), and the checker refuses an extender at another type.** It builds P-146. A self-typed trait is a trait `C[\X\]` whose parameter `X` extends `C[\X\]`. The Return Type check reads `self` as `C[\X\] & X` in the trait's signature where an object's dotted method redeclares the trait's method at the same parameter types, so it refuses a narrower return type; a getter so redeclared reads the plain type and compiles (F-394). That reading of a dotted method departs from P-146 and P-157, which keep the plain signatures for the Return Type Rule (row 1094). The checker also takes a parameter bounded by a self-typed trait that the trait extends at that parameter, as the team's `Containment` mixins are: a default of the coordinator's. In a trait whose `comprises` clause lists types, `self` is instead the trait met with the union of those types, and the exclusion test reads its signatures plain. For example, `object Bad extends Integral[\ZZ32\]` is refused, since `Bad` is not a `ZZ32`. The rule accepts every self-typed body of the library but `Integral`'s `numerator`, which each integer type declares instead (P-146). Walk has no such rule and loads what it refuses. A compiled default body that returns `self` at the self-type parameter runs, also where the parameter is an object type, as in `object Pt(…) extends Shape[\Pt\]` (row 695, fixed). F-377 gives the cast that does it, also at a conditional's arm (`StandardTotalOrder`'s `MIN` and `MAX`) and at a closure's result (rows 1051 and 1050, fixed). A default body that calls a method on a value of the self-type parameter runs, also where the object lists the trait first (row 1052, fixed); F-385 gives how, and the generic object for which it may not hold. The rule refuses, rightly, a team declaration, `PureList`'s `List` (row 355). The compiler's prelude writes the self type instead as `comprises T` on `StandardTotalOrder` and `Equality` (`Library/CompilerAlgebra.fsi:16`, `:24`), and that spelling does not bind the one library, since Naden's last text puts the self-type idiom in bounds only, not as a first-class type (`Papers/Types/journal/justificationOfRTR.tex:626-630`), and Steele's own message on `655e65d53` gives the self type as the trait met with the `comprises` clause, which this rule reads from the F-bound. Gated by `compiler_tests/SelfType*` and `XXXSelfType*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-self-type/REPORT.md` sections 1, 2, 4 and 7; `compile-ladder/rung-self-type-exclusion/REPORT.md` section 1.
  from: reviews/batch-34-review.md, "Findings for the record, with no decision of his changed"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: brought in line with the tree at row 1094, opened by rung G of climb batch 34 and older than the batch: the Return Type check reads a dotted method's receiver from the index's self type (OverloadingChecker.scala:214) and checks the pair both ways (:733-734), on the base and after the rung; a getter reads the plain type (getterArrow, :224). To make room, moved here as how the facts came about: the prelude spells the self type on StandardTotalOrder since Steele's 655e65d53, 2012-06-05, and 59fdeff62, 2012-06-06, which took its F-bound away; the demo trips.fss's generator, which the rule refused, extends StandardTotalOrder[\generator\] since its repair (row 710)

note P-102
  from: reviews/batch-34-review.md, "The finding for the curator"; from: coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-11-18)"; commit: 285efad8021dce9f3fa685ddc19a04660e0b69d5; note: his words in chat at 15:49:11 UTC on 2026-11-18, on the worker's test, which the block of 2026-11-20 14:38Z does not hold (it gives his words of 15:4x that day on a refactor only): "you should be writing the test case, state what the expected failure is, then run it. If it matches your expected failure, then you can start working on the implementation. If it fails for a different reason than you expected, you need to deal with those cases", and his question "would this statement of expected failure before the run be also checkable by skeptic?". The rule stands in the fortress-repo skill's references/tests-writing.md, "The order", step 2; the batch manual's skeptic check 2 also accepts the failure that the section names, a fallback that his words do not have (climb-batch-workflow.md:128). P-102's text is kept.
